+Repeats primitives in polar coordinates (cylindrical symmetry) rather than Cartesian. Choose when you need radial tiling (e.g., rings, spokes) instead of grid tiling.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per query. The operation involves a constant number of arithmetic operations regardless of scene complexity [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$. The state is purely functional with no additional data structures stored [G].
-- **Strengths:** Extremely efficient for creating infinite environments or dense grids without increasing the number of primitives in the scene graph [S4, unofficial]. It reuses a single primitive definition [S4, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Only supports rectangular grid tiling [G]. Normal calculation requires care at tile boundaries due to potential gradient discontinuities [G].
-- **When to use:** When an infinite grid of identical objects is needed (e.g., a floor, a wall of pillars, a starfield) [S2, unofficial][S4, unofficial].
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per query, identical to the base primitive. The modulo operation is a constant-time arithmetic operation.
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$. No additional data structures are stored.
+- **Strengths:** Infinite instances with zero memory overhead. Ideal for floors, walls, or abstract patterns where geometry is repetitive.
+- **Limitations:** Only supports uniform, axis-aligned grid repetition. Cannot handle non-uniform scaling or rotation of the repetition grid without additional transformations. Artifacts may appear if the base primitive is not symmetric or if the cell size is not a multiple of the primitive's extent.
+- **When to use:** When rendering infinite grids of identical objects (e.g., a tiled floor, a field of spheres).
+- **When not to use:** When objects need to interact with each other (e.g., collision between adjacent instances) or when the repetition pattern is non-uniform.
 
 ## Background
-Space Repetition is a fundamental technique in Signed Distance Field (SDF) raymarching, popularized by online SDF tutorials and GLSL shader communities [S4, unofficial]. It is a direct application of modulo arithmetic to coordinate space [S3, unofficial]. The technique is often referred to as "cheating" because it avoids the computational cost of rendering multiple distinct objects by reusing a single SDF evaluation [S4, unofficial].
+Space Repetition is a staple technique in Signed Distance Field (SDF) raymarching, popularized by tutorials on GPU raymarching and distance functions. It is often referred to as "cheating" because it allows the rendering of infinite geometry without increasing the scene's geometric complexity [S5, unofficial]. The technique relies on the periodicity of the modulo operator to fold space.
 
